    "media:imagetext": "2/\nBaguio, P. I.\nDec. 17, 1937\nMr. S. W. Morgan,\nPan American Airways Company,\nNew York.\nDear Stokeley:\nI have your memorandum attached to clipping from the New York\nTimes under Shanghai date line of November 23rd, the article appearing over\nthe name of Hallett Abend.\nIf you have not done so would suggest that you\nread Abend's book \"Can China Survive\" which I know you will find very much\nworthwhile. In that book in which Abend colloborated with Anthony J. Billing-\nit was pointed out that the Communists and many war lords in outlying\nprovinces would certainly revolt against the Central Government in the event\nof a war with Japan and numerous other predictions were made none of which\nhas been true and all of which were slightly cynical with respect to the\nChinese in general. In other words I think Abend is possibly over critical.\nin my recent trip to Nanking I learned from the best authority\nnat in vuly chiang kai-shek called in all of the important provincial an\nwilling to support the National Government in the conduct of such a war.\nThe unanimous verdict was in favor of war inless Japanese agression ceased.\nThe question was then asked by the Generalissimo of the Southern and other\nleaders whether or not they would continue to support the Central Government\nafter the loss of Shanghai. There was no hesitancy whatever on the part of the\ngroup present in accepting\nthis possibility and assuring complete support.\nThe question was then asked whether they would continue to support the Central\nGovernment following the loss of Nanking and again the support was unanimous.\nIf we can accept this statement as reasonably reliable it seems to me that the\nme at least that if the Chinese morale does not collapse that their position\nis getting stronger all the time while the Japanese position is becoming more\nprecarious.\nIn other words I felt in the beginning and have seen no reason\nsince to change my opinion, that the Japanese strategy would be to take North.\nChina, blocade all Chinese ports, seize Shanghai and then stand thus compelling\nthe Chinese to carry the war against well defended Japanese positions at points\nchosen by the Japanese as the most favorable to defend.\nThis would have\ncreated a situation where the cost to the Japanese would have been reduced to\na minimum and the Chinese would have been compelled to attack, a form of war-\nfare with which they are not familiar.\nAs against this strategy the Japanese\nit seems to me have made a great mistake in following the Chinese inland. It\nis now necessary for the Japanese to maintain aprecarious service of supply\nover a very wide area and to maintain a front line which in the Nanking area\nalone will be more than 500 miles in length. If the present rumors are true\nthat the Japs expect to attempt to take Canton it seems to me that this will\nbring about a unification such as China has never known and will in turn fur-\nther weaken the Japanese position.\nCommentint now upon Abend's statement to the effect that Chinese\nthat the\npossibly lost view of the fact that the Chinese soldier is accustomed to hard-",
